        <div id="post-773" class="post-773 post type-post hentry category-wpf">

          <div class="post-header">
           <h3 class="post-title"><a href="http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/2010/03/grouping-is-crazy-easy-in-wpf/" rel="bookmark" title="Permanent Link: Grouping is crazy easy in WPF">Grouping is crazy easy in WPF</a></h3>
           <p class="post-date">
            <span class="month">Mar</span>
            <span class="day">18</span>
           </p>
           <p class="post-author">
            <span class="info">Posted by <a href="http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/author/cromwellryan/" title="Posts by cromwellryan ">cromwellryan</a> in <a href="http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/category/wpf/" title="View all posts in WPF" rel="category tag">WPF</a> | <a href="http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/2010/03/grouping-is-crazy-easy-in-wpf/#respond" class="comments"  title="Comment on Grouping is crazy easy in WPF"><span class="dsq-postid" rel="773 http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/index.php/2010/03/grouping-is-crazy-easy-in-wpf/">View Comments</span></a>              </span>
           </p>
          </div>

          <div class="post-content clearfix">
          <p>Lots of people struggle with grouping data and when they do, the brut force method normally takes over and we start creating some crazy super structure to organize data and then do something like put a List inside of a List or a Grid inside of a list.&#160; Just think of how many times you [...]</p>

