        <div id="post-737" class="post-737 post type-post hentry category-net category-wpf">

          <div class="post-header">
           <h3 class="post-title"><a href="http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/2009/07/property-undo-redo-support/" rel="bookmark" title="Permanent Link: Property Undo/Redo Support">Property Undo/Redo Support</a></h3>
           <p class="post-date">
            <span class="month">Jul</span>
            <span class="day">4</span>
           </p>
           <p class="post-author">
            <span class="info">Posted by <a href="http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/author/cromwellryan/" title="Posts by cromwellryan ">cromwellryan</a> in <a href="http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/category/net/" title="View all posts in .Net" rel="category tag">.Net</a>, <a href="http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/category/wpf/" title="View all posts in WPF" rel="category tag">WPF</a> | <a href="http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/2009/07/property-undo-redo-support/#respond" class="comments"  title="Comment on Property Undo/Redo Support"><span class="dsq-postid" rel="737 /blogs/ryanc/archive/2009/07/04/property-undo-redo-support.aspx">View Comments</span></a>              </span>
           </p>
          </div>

          <div class="post-content clearfix">
          <p>Undo/Redo support is one of those golden features that really differentiate a client app from many web apps.&#160; There have been a number of methods/techniques to provide this support that I’ve run across from brute force, to the memento pattern, but none that lit any fire for me. Here’s my answer to the problem using [...]</p>

